{"author":"Rebar Bot","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Problem\n`aggregate.py`'s `_severity_rank`/`_SEVERITY_RANK` (an ordinal string->int map over the\n5-level severity vocabulary) picks the representative finding in a duplicate cluster and\nsorts the final merged list, but the numeric `priority` (`validity x impact`) computed by\n`pass3_decide` is dropped before findings reach `aggregate.py`: `shim.py`'s\n`_to_common_finding` only carries `severity` forward, not `priority`. This is the exact bug\nmotivating the epic: a high-impact/low-validity finding can rank above a genuinely\nhigh-priority one because ranking is impact-only.\n\n`aggregate_findings` is a public library function (re-exported at `rebar.llm.aggregate_findings`)\nwith no internal caller in this repo, so its actual callers -- inside this pipeline and any\nexternal consumer -- are not fully known/controlled; a bare `finding[\"priority\"]` subscript\nwould KeyError for any finding lacking the key. `_severity_rank`/`_SEVERITY_RANK` themselves\nmust NOT be deleted: `spec_scan.py` imports and calls `_severity_rank` directly, on its own\nfindings (from a different LLM reviewer that never runs through `pass3_decide` and never gets\n`priority`) -- that producer's own priority migration is a separate sibling ticket\n(broken-spirited-dorado), out of scope here.\n\n## Approach\n1. Thread `priority` (and `decision`) through `code_review/shim.py`'s `_to_common_finding` so\n   the common-finding shape `aggregate.py` consumes carries the numeric value already computed\n   by `pass3_decide` (or set on DET-tier findings by the sibling priority-backfill ticket).\n2. In `aggregate.py`, change `aggregate_findings`'s ranking key (used for both representative\n   selection and the final sort) to read `float(finding.get(\"priority\") or 0.0)` -- a safe\n   numeric default for any finding lacking the key -- instead of `_severity_rank(finding)`.\n   Do NOT delete `_severity_rank`/`_SEVERITY_RANK`: they stay defined, unchanged, for\n   `spec_scan.py`'s continued independent use.\n3. `spec_scan.py` is explicitly OUT OF SCOPE for this ticket -- it is not touched here.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] `shim.py::_to_common_finding` includes `priority` in its output dict for every finding.\n- [ ] `aggregate.py::aggregate_findings` selects the cluster representative and sorts the\n  final list by `float(finding.get(\"priority\") or 0.0)` (ties broken as today by detail\n  length); a finding lacking `priority` ranks as `0.0` rather than raising.\n- [ ] `_severity_rank` and `_SEVERITY_RANK` remain defined, unchanged, in `aggregate.py`\n  (`grep -n \"_severity_rank\\|_SEVERITY_RANK\" src/rebar/llm/aggregate.py` still finds them).\n- [ ] `src/rebar/llm/spec_scan.py` has zero diff from this ticket\n  (`git diff --stat src/rebar/llm/spec_scan.py` is empty).\n- [ ] `pytest tests/unit/test_llm_aggregate.py` (locate via\n  `pytest --collect-only -q -k aggregate`) passes, with a strengthened test asserting a\n  higher-impact/lower-validity finding no longer wins representative selection over a\n  lower-impact/higher-validity one with higher priority, AND a new test asserting a finding\n  with no `priority` key ranks at `0.0` without raising.\n\n## Testing\nTier: in-process unit test against the real `aggregate_findings`/representative-selection\nfunction, fed production-shaped finding dicts (contract test: real producer shape -> real\nconsumer). Oracle: which finding is selected as representative and the sort order of the\nmerged list, asserted by finding identity. Contrast cases: (1) a high-impact/low-validity\nfinding (low priority) paired with a moderate-impact/high-validity one (higher priority) --\nassert the second wins; (2) a finding dict with no `priority` key at all -- assert it ranks\nat `0.0` and the call does not raise `KeyError`/`TypeError`."}},"env_id":"eea68b7f-6697-488b-ac52-744e2ed8e96f","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1788127059286514246,"uuid":"f74d6c86-de7c-4186-8515-56704c7d65fb"}